WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.500
<v Instructor>Okay, so let's go ahead</v>

2
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.560
and practice using the Beanstalk service.

3
00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.560
So I'm gonna go into the Elastic Beanstalk console

4
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.740
and we're going to create our first application.

5
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.210
So we have an option

6
00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.400
to choose either a web server environment

7
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.500
or a worker environment.

8
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.030
So right now we want to run a website,

9
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:19.950
so we'll choose web server environment,

10
00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.890
but if we wanted to process tasks off of a queue,

11
00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.320
then we would choose a worker environment.

12
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.420
But we will only do web server.

13
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.310
So let's create an application,

14
00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:32.610
and this one is called My Application.

15
00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:33.540
Okay.

16
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.320
So next we scroll down and we have environment information.

17
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.060
So the environment is sort of filled,

18
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.810
but I'm just going to call this one My Application Dev,

19
00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.280
because this is going to represent

20
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.320
my development environment.

21
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.050
A domain name is going to be automatically generated

22
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:50.070
for my application,

23
00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:53.010
and this is how I will access my web servers.

24
00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.540
Okay, so we scroll down

25
00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.670
and now we need to choose a platform.

26
00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.010
So this platform is going to be managed

27
00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.500
and I will choose no JS,

28
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.240
and then I will just choose the default options.

29
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.860
You may see something different than me,

30
00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.200
but by using the latest defaults,

31
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.420
you should be fine and good to go.

32
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.720
Next, we need to choose some application code.

33
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.130
So I'll use a sample application.

34
00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:15.477
We can definitely approve our own code,

35
00:01:15.477 --> 00:01:17.130
but we don't have that right now,

36
00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.243
and so this sample application

37
00:01:18.243 --> 00:01:22.380
will be matching the environment I have right here.

38
00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:23.580
Okay, so let's scroll down.

39
00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.590
We have presets, and Beanstalk

40
00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.620
can be quite complicated for the configuration, and as such,

41
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.100
we can set recommended values for either a single instance,

42
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.860
which is free tier eligible, or high availability,

43
00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.140
where we have a load balancer for example,

44
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.220
or custom configuration,

45
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.080
if you wanted to customize everything.

46
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.520
Now to keep things simple,

47
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.070
we're going to go over single instance.

48
00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.900
So you click on next.

49
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.450
Next you have to configure the service access.

50
00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:52.020
So right now, no roles are available for me here,

51
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:55.350
so I'm going to just create a role for a service role.

52
00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:59.790
So it's for a service. This is for a Beanstalk environment.

53
00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:03.240
We click on Next. We have these permission policies.

54
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.650
We click on Next,

55
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.620
and the name is AWS Elastic Beanstalk service role.

56
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.160
So this is all pre-filled for us. Let's create this role.

57
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.900
So now this role is created,

58
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.420
and I can refresh and select it here.

59
00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.500
That's perfect.

60
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.660
Now for the EC2 instance profile, this is the same thing.

61
00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.253
We're going to create a role,

62
00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.433
and now this is for Beanstalk Compute.

63
00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:25.800
Click on Next.

64
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.620
We have all these things already added to this role,

65
00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:29.453
so that's perfect.

66
00:02:29.453 --> 00:02:32.670
Click on Next, scroll down, click on Create Role.

67
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:34.590
The role is not created.

68
00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.370
Back in here, we refresh and we're good to go.

69
00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:41.370
This is optional, so we will leave it empty.

70
00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.100
And finally, we could configure networking, and so on,

71
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.470
step three, four, five, but we don't need to,

72
00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.720
so we're just going to skip to review

73
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.360
and click on it to go straight to the review page,

74
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.970
because we just want to use the defaults that were set

75
00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.890
for the single instance mode.

76
00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.420
So make sure under service access

77
00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.460
that you do have the service role

78
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.950
and the EC2 instance profile selected.

79
00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:03.360
And when you're good to go,

80
00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.760
you can definitely look at those, but they're complicated.

81
00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.960
Click on Submit,

82
00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.653
and this will go and create our first Beanstalk environment.

83
00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.470
So now if we scroll down under Events,

84
00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.140
as we can see, some events are happening,

85
00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.080
and these events actually come from a service,

86
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.240
called CloudFormation.

87
00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.240
So if you go into the CloudFormation console,

88
00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.110
we can see our Elastic Beanstalk stack right here.

89
00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:31.080
So I'm going to just go on the right hand side

90
00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.730
and make it bigger.

91
00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:35.820
So under Events, if you're clicking fast enough,

92
00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.970
you will still see that stuff is being created in progress,

93
00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:40.890
and then once stuff is done being created,

94
00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.480
it goes to Create Complete.

95
00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.450
So this is the line of events of all the things happening

96
00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.460
within your CloudFormation template,

97
00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.820
and all the things that is being created by CloudFormation.

98
00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.470
CloudFormation is a service we'll see later on,

99
00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:54.060
but here under Resources,

100
00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.490
we can see we have created a auto scaling group.

101
00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.640
We have created a launch configuration, and so on,

102
00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.350
and Elastic IP, and so on.

103
00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.720
One thing you can look at is under templates,

104
00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:07.260
you can view the templates in Application Composer.

105
00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.050
And the cool thing about it is that we can visually see

106
00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.990
what is being created by a CloudFormation.

107
00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:14.100
So we can see, for example,

108
00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.260
that we have a launch configuration,

109
00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:19.050
we have security groups, we have an elastic IP,

110
00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.210
a weight condition, and a condition handle.

111
00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.970
So this is very handy to start visualizing

112
00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:27.840
what Elastic Beanstalk is creating for us behind the scenes.

113
00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.480
So now let's go back into Elastic Beanstalk.

114
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.930
So in the Events tab, as we can see now,

115
00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:34.830
we have some information.

116
00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:38.460
For example, a security group was created,

117
00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.730
and then an Elastic IP was created.

118
00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:45.060
Then we wait for the EC2 instances to launch,

119
00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.160
and then the instances will be created.

120
00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:52.160
So if I switch now and I go under EC2, the EC2 console,

121
00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:56.550
and look at my instances running, actually yes,

122
00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:01.550
one of my instances is running, it's using a T-3 micro.

123
00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.270
And as we can see,

124
00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:06.780
this instance has a public IP address, right here.

125
00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.960
And if I go on the left hand side under Elastic IPs,

126
00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.270
we can see that this elastic IP address

127
00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:16.980
was created and is allocated to our EC2 instance.

128
00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.260
And if I go under auto scaling groups,

129
00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:22.290
we can see that an auto scaling group was created,

130
00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:25.650
and this auto scaling group, under Instance Management,

131
00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.200
is managing my only EC2 instance.

132
00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.690
That's why it's called a single EC2 instance.

133
00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:32.850
So all of this makes sense,

134
00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.460
and that's what Beanstalk is created.

135
00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.890
And at the end of it, when everything is launched,

136
00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.780
you'll say, okay, successfully launched,

137
00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.090
and it will be okay for health.

138
00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:44.490
And when we get out of it is a domain name.

139
00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:47.880
So if I click on this domain name and open it in a new tab,

140
00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.960
I get, actually, access to my Beanstalk environment

141
00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.097
and my single EC2 instance, and it says,

142
00:05:54.097 --> 00:05:57.240
"Congratulations, you are now running Elastic Beanstalk

143
00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:00.090
on this EC2 instance," which is amazing.

144
00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.310
So you can see very simply,

145
00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:05.430
Beanstalk was creating for us, just from the code,

146
00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.740
the sample code that we were using,

147
00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.260
it generated all the infrastructure

148
00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:12.870
to start successfully my application,

149
00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:15.870
my web server, which is very, very good.

150
00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.730
So now let's have a look at a couple of options.

151
00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:20.280
So if we wanted to upload a new version,

152
00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.830
we click on here, we could upload a new version,

153
00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:27.300
and automatically, it would be deployed to my EC2 instances.

154
00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.030
So we don't have that, but just to give you an overview.

155
00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:31.800
Health gives you some information

156
00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:33.750
around the health checks of all your instances,

157
00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.340
if you had many.

158
00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:39.030
You can go under logs to view the logs of your application.

159
00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:40.170
You can go on monitoring

160
00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.930
to have a look at all the metrics for your application.

161
00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.690
So we get really like some view-centric

162
00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.270
around all my application itself.

163
00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:51.720
The alarms manage updates, which is when Beanstalk,

164
00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.630
the sets you update, our entire environment.

165
00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:57.000
And then if you click on configuration,

166
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.120
you can actually have a look at all the configuration

167
00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:03.510
of your Beanstalk environment, modify them and apply them,

168
00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:06.420
which are quite well-rounded,

169
00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:08.610
but we don't need to know about this right now.

170
00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:12.810
Now, more importantly, if we go under My Application,

171
00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.660
as we can see right here, there's My Application Dev.

172
00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:17.160
That's one environment,

173
00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:20.160
but I could go ahead and create a second environment,

174
00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.220
for example, My Application Prod, and that will allow us

175
00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:26.400
to really think about environments overall.

176
00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.450
So that's it for an overview of Beanstalk.

177
00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:35.450
So to reason about it, Beanstalk is centered around code,

178
00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.710
and environments for your code, whereas,

179
00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:39.660
if you wanted to look at CloudFormation,

180
00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:41.760
the service we had seen before,

181
00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.210
CloudFormation is used to deploy stacks arbitrarily

182
00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:47.910
with any kind of infrastructure.

183
00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.730
So we've seen all that Beanstalk has created,

184
00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:53.010
including my auto scanning group, my EC2 instance,

185
00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:54.780
the Elastic IP, and so on,

186
00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.330
and so if you are in a course

187
00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:59.370
that has more Beanstalk lectures,

188
00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:02.190
so for example the Certified Developer Course,

189
00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:03.900
then do not delete your application.

190
00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.370
We'll be using it more.

191
00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:08.700
But if you are done with the Beanstalk lectures,

192
00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:10.620
you've known enough for the exam,

193
00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:13.080
so what you can do is take your application,

194
00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.780
do action, and then delete application,

195
00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.340
and then you will have cleaned up.

196
00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:18.180
So that's it.

197
00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:21.633
Make your own call, and I will see you in the next lecture.

